  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Minimal pre-Thanksgiving travel weather impacts \n\nA cold front moving west to east across the Commonwealth will\nbring some light rain to parts of CPA early today particularly\nin the Laurel Highlands. A colder NW flow behind the front \nreinforced by a fast moving shortwave trough crossing the Great \nLakes will promote the development of rain and snow showers \ntonight downwind of Lake Erie. The lake effect streamer bands \ncould potentially extend to the I-80 corridor overnight into \nearly Tuesday morning. Lowering inversion heights in response to\neastward migrating 1030+mb high pressure will bring a quick end\nto the lake effect precip early in the day on Tuesday, leading \nto a 24-36 hour period of quiet/dry weather and good holiday \ntravel conditions. Tuesday night may be the coldest night of the\nseason so far as mainly clear, Beaver Supermoon-lit skies and \nlight winds will allow min temps to fall into the 15 to 25F \nrange. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Wet and windy Thanksgiving Day\n\nWidespread soaking 1+ inch rainfall expected on Thanksgiving \nDay as a very strong easterly low level flow associated with a \npowerful holiday coastal storm system transports anomalous \nmoisture into CPA. Model consensus timing suggests precip\ncrosses the MD line around or shortly after 12Z/7AM Thursday, \nbut given the surge of warm air advection would not be surprised\nif an earlier onset occurs. Depending on how fast the \nprecipitation arrives, there may still be enough retreating cold\nair in place to support a mix of rain and snow on the front-end\nbefore changing to all rain by the afternoon. The heaviest rain\nis projected to fall in the 12-18hr window between 7AM Thu and \n1AM Fri. \n\nThe potential for strong wind gusts remains a concern with this\nsystem, particularly over the southeastern third of the CWA. \nRecent runs of some of the deterministic models, including the \nGFS, ECMWF, AIGFS, and the AIFS have shown a 60 to 80 knot 850 \nmb low-level jet developing in response to the strong \ntemperature/pressure gradient between the deepening low to the \nsouth and retreating high pressure to the north. It remains to \nbe seen how much of this wind will be able to make it down to \nthe surface, but soundings from the GFS show 45-50 knot winds \nat the top of the mixed layer Thursday afternoon over the Lower \nSusquehanna Valley. The NBM 24 hour maximum wind gust product \nalso shows better than 50/50 odds of wind gusts exceeding 45 \nmph or advisory criteria over Lancaster County. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Breezy Black Friday with rain/elevation snow  \n\nColder air will gradually wrap back into Pennsylvania late \nThursday night and Friday as the system stacks up along the\nNorth-Central Appalachians. This should allow for snow to begin\nmixing in especially over the higher terrain from the Laurel\nHighlands northward along the Allegheny Plateau into northern\nPA. Rain/snow mix during the day Friday beneath the stacked low\ntransitions to more of a lake effect/upslope precip pattern \nFriday night into Saturday as the system lifts into New England.\nIt will remain breezy into the holiday weekend with west winds \ngusting 25-35 mph.",
